/*!
    \page qtbridges-python-api.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Python API
    \brief API Startup Guide

    This guide provides a comprehensive overview of the QtBridges API for integrating Python with
    QML. It covers the main functions, decorators, and patterns you'll use to build QML applications
    with Python backends.

    \section1 Core Concepts

    QtBridges provides two main approaches for integrating Python with QML:

    \list 1
        \li \b {Instance Registration} (\c {bridge_instance()}): Expose existing Python objects to
            QML as singletons
        \li \b {Type Registration} (\c {bridge_type()}): Register Python classes so QML can
            instantiate them
    \endlist

    \section2 When to Use What?

    \table
    \header
        \li Use Case
        \li Recommend API
    \row
        \li Expose data models to QML (ListView, TableView)
        \li \c {bridge_instance()}
    \row
        \li Application state/settings that QML reads
        \li \c {bridge_instance()}
    \row
        \li QML needs to create multiple instances
        \li \c {bridge_type()}
    \row
        \li Reusable QML components backed by Python
        \li \c {bridge_type()}
    \endtable


    \section1 API Overview

    \section2 Core Functions

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import (
        bridge_instance,  # Expose Python object as QML singleton
        bridge_type,      # Register Python class for QML instantiation
        qtbridge,         # Application entry point decorator

        # Model update decorators
        insert,           # Mark insert operations
        remove,           # Mark remove operations
        edit,             # Mark edit operations
        move,             # Mark move operations
        reset,            # Mark reset operations
        complete,         # Mark component initialization (for bridge_type)
    )
    \endcode

    \section1 bridge_instance()

    Registers a Python object instance as a QML singleton. The object becomes accessible from QML by
    name.

    \section2 Signature

    \code [python]
    @overload
    def bridge_instance(instance: DataProvider, name: str, uri: str = "backend") -> None:
        """
        Registers a Python class instance as a QML singleton bridge.

        Parameters
        ----------
        instance : DataProvider
            A Python object implementing a `data()` method.
        name : str
            The QML model name (must start with uppercase letter).
        uri : str, optional
            The QML module URI under which the singleton will be registered.
            Defaults to "backend".
        """
    ...

    @overload
    def bridge_instance(obj: list | tuple | numpy.ndarray, name: str, uri: str = "backend") -> None:
        """
        Registers a Python container (list, tuple) as a QML model.

        Parameters
        ----------
        obj : list | tuple | numpy.ndarray
            The Python container / numpy array to expose to QML.
        name : str
            The QML model name (must start with uppercase letter).
        uri : str, optional
            The QML module URI. Defaults to "backend".
        """
        ...
    \endcode

    \section2 Parameters

    \list
        \li \b {instance}: Python object with a `data()` method that returns the
            underlying data
        \li \b {name}: Name to use in QML (must start with uppercase, e.g., "UserModel")
        \li \b {uri}: QML module namespace (default: `"backend"`, can be custom like
            `"myapp.models"`)
    \endlist

    \section2 Requirements

    The Python class must implement a \c {data()} method with a return type hint:

    \code [python]
    def data(self) -> list[str]:  # Type hint is required!
        return self._items
    \endcode

    \section2 Example: Simple List Model

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import bridge_instance, insert, remove, qtbridge

    class TodoList:
        def __init__(self):
            self._todos = ["Buy groceries", "Walk the dog"]

        @insert
        def add_todo(self, task: str) -> bool:
            self._todos.append(task)
            return True

        @remove
        def remove_todo(self, index: int) -> bool:
            if 0 <= index < len(self._todos):
                self._todos.pop(index)
                return True
            return False

        def data(self) -> list[str]:
            return self._todos

    @qtbridge(module="Main")
    def main():
        todo_list = TodoList()
        bridge_instance(todo_list, name="TodoList")
    \endcode

    \b {QML Usage:}

    \code [qml]
    import QtQuick
    import backend 1.0

    ListView {
        model: TodoList
        delegate: Text { text: modelData }
    }

    Button {
        text: "Add Task"
        onClicked: TodoList.add_todo("New task")
    }
    \endcode

    \section2 Example: Custom URI

    \code [python]
    bridge_instance(user_model, name="UserModel", uri="myapp.models")
    \endode

    \code [qml]
    import myapp.models 1.0

    ListView {
        model: UserModel
    }
    \endcode

    \section2 Example: DataClass Models

    \code [python]
    from dataclasses import dataclass
    from QtBridge import bridge_instance

    @dataclass
    class User:
        name: str
        email: str
        age: int

    class UserDatabase:
        def __init__(self):
            self._users = [
                User("Alice", "alice@example.com", 30),
                User("Bob", "bob@example.com", 25),
            ]

        def data(self) -> list[User]:  # Type hint with dataclass
            return self._users

    # Register the instance
    db = UserDatabase()
    bridge_instance(db, name="UserDB")
    \endcode

    \b {QML Access:}

    \code [qml]
    import backend 1.0

    ListView {
        model: UserDB
        delegate: Column {
            Text { text: "Name: " + modelData.name }
            Text { text: "Email: " + modelData.email }
            Text { text: "Age: " + modelData.age }
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \section1 bridge_type()

    Registers a Python class as a QML type, allowing QML to instantiate it directly.

    \section2 Signature

    \code [python]
    def bridge_type(
        type: type,
        uri: str | None = None,
        version: str | None = None,
        name: str | None = None,
        default_property: str | None = None
    ) -> None:
        """
        Registers a Python class as a QML type.

        Parameters
        ----------
        type : type
            The Python class type to register as a QML type.
        uri : str, optional
            The QML module name under which the type will be available.
            If not provided, the type won't be importable from QML.
        version : str, optional
            QML version in "major.minor" format, e.g. "1.0".
            Defaults to "1.0" if not specified.
        name : str, optional
            Custom name to use for the QML type. Defaults to the Python
            class name if not provided.
        default_property : str, optional
            The name of a property in the Python class to be treated as the
            default property in QML (for child element assignment).
        """
    \endcode

    \section2 Parameters

    \list
        \li \b {type}: The Python class to register
        \li \b {uri}: QML module namespace (e.g., `"backend"`, `"myapp.ui"`)
        \li \b {version}: Version string (e.g., `"1.0"`, `"2.5"`)
        \li \b {name}: Custom QML type name (defaults to class name)
        \li \b {default_property}: Property name for default child assignment in QML
    \endlist

    \section2 Example: Basic Type Registration

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import bridge_type, qtbridge

    class Counter:
        def __init__(self):
            self._count = 0

        @property
        def count(self) -> int:
            return self._count

        @count.setter
        def count(self, value: int):
            self._count = value

        def increment(self) -> None:
            self._count += 1

        def data(self) -> list[int]:  # Required for bridge compatibility
            return [self._count]

    @qtbridge(module="Main")
    def main():
        bridge_type(Counter, uri="backend", version="1.0")
    \endcode

    \b {QML Usage:}

    \code [qml]
    import QtQuick
    import backend 1.0

    Item {
        Counter {
            id: counter1
            count: 5
        }

        Counter {
            id: counter2
            count: 10
        }

        Text {
            text: "Total: " + (counter1.count + counter2.count)
        }

        Button {
            text: "Increment Counter 1"
            onClicked: counter1.increment()
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \section2 Example: Default Property

    The \c {default_property} parameter allows QML to assign child elements without explicitly
    naming the property.

    \code [python]
    from typing import Optional
    from QtBridge import bridge_type

    class Container:
        def __init__(self):
            self._child: Optional[Widget] = None

        @property
        def child(self) -> Optional['Widget']:
            return self._child

        @child.setter
        def child(self, widget: Optional['Widget']):
            self._child = widget

    class Widget:
        def __init__(self):
            self._text = ""

        @property
        def text(self) -> str:
            return self._text

        @text.setter
        def text(self, value: str):
            self._text = value


    # Register both types
    bridge_type(Widget, uri="backend", version="1.0")
    bridge_type(Container, uri="backend", version="1.0", default_property="child")
    \endcode

    \b {QML Usage:}

    \code [qml]
    import backend 1.0

    Container {
        // Widget is automatically assigned to the 'child' property
        Widget {
            text: "I'm the default child!"
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \section1 @qtbridge Decorator

    The \c {@qtbridge} decorator provides a convenient way to set up your QML application with
    automatic engine initialization and QML loading.

    \section2 Signature

    \code [python]
    def qtbridge(
        module: str | None = None,
        type_name: str | None = None,
        qml_file: str | None = None,
        import_paths: List[str] | None = None
    ) -> Callable[[Callable[..., None]], Callable[..., None]]:
        """
        Decorator for the main function to set up QML application.

        Parameters
        ----------
        module : str, optional
            QML module file name (without extension). E.g., "Main" loads "Main.qml"
        type_name : str, optional
            Specific QML type name to load from the module.
        qml_file : str, optional
            Direct path to a QML file to load.
        import_paths : List[str], optional
            Additional directories to search for QML modules.

        Notes
        -----
        - Automatically creates QGuiApplication and QQmlApplicationEngine
        - Loads the specified QML file
        - Handles the Qt event loop
        """
    \endcode

    \section2 Parameters

    \list
        \li \b {module}: QML module name (looks for `<module>.qml` in the same directory)
        \li \b {type_name}: Specific QML component to instantiate
        \li \b {qml_file}: Direct path to QML file (alternative to `module`)
        \li \b {import_paths}: Additional directories for QML imports
    \endlist

    \section2 Example: Basic Usage

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import bridge_instance, qtbridge

    class AppModel:
        def __init__(self):
            self._title = "My Application"

        @property
        def title(self) -> str:
            return self._title

        def data(self) -> list:
            return []

    @qtbridge(module="Main")  # Loads Main.qml in the same directory
    def main():
        app_model = AppModel()
        bridge_instance(app_model, name="AppModel")

    if __name__ == "__main__":
        main()
    \endcode

    \section2 Example: Custom QML Path

    \code [python]
    @qtbridge(qml_file="ui/MainWindow.qml")
    def main():
        # Setup code
        pass
    \endcode

    \section2 Example: Custom Import Paths

    \code [python]
    @qtbridge(
        module="Main",
        import_paths=["./qml_components", "./resources"]
    )
    def main():
        # Setup code
        pass
    \endcode

    \section1 Model Update Decorators

    QtBridges provides decorators to mark methods that modify data models. These decorators
    automatically notify QML when data changes, triggering UI updates.

    \section2 @insert

    Marks a method that inserts new items into the model.

    \code [python]
    def insert(func: Callable[..., Any]) -> Callable[..., Any]:
        """
        Decorator that marks a method as an *insert* operation for QML-bound
        list or table models.
        """
    \endcode

    \b {Example:}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import insert

    class TaskList:
        def __init__(self):
            self._tasks = []

        @insert
        def add_task(self, task: str) -> bool:
            """Insert method should return bool indicating success"""
            self._tasks.append(task)
            return True

        @insert
        def insert_at(self, index: int, task: str) -> bool:
            """Can insert at specific positions"""
            if 0 <= index <= len(self._tasks):
                self._tasks.insert(index, task)
                return True
            return False

        def data(self) -> list[str]:
            return self._tasks
    \endcode

    \section2 @remove

    Marks a method that removes items from the model.

    \code [python]
    def remove(func: Callable[..., Any]) -> Callable[..., Any]:
        """
        Decorator that marks a method as a *remove* operation for QML-bound
        models.
        """
    \endcode

    \b {Example:}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import remove

    class TaskList:
        def __init__(self):
            self._tasks = ["Task 1", "Task 2"]

        @remove
        def remove_task(self, index: int) -> bool:
            """Remove by index"""
            if 0 <= index < len(self._tasks):
                self._tasks.pop(index)
                return True
            return False

        @remove
        def clear_all(self) -> bool:
            """Remove all items"""
            self._tasks.clear()
            return True

        def data(self) -> list[str]:
            return self._tasks
    \endcode

    \section2 @edit

    Marks a method that modifies existing items in the model.

    \code [python]
    def edit(func: Callable[..., Any]) -> Callable[..., Any]:
        """
        Decorator that marks a method as an *edit* operation for QML-bound
        models.
        """
    \endcode

    \b {Example:}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import edit

    class TaskList:
        def __init__(self):
            self._tasks = ["Buy milk", "Walk dog"]

        @edit
        def update_task(self, index: int, new_text: str) -> bool:
            """Update task at index"""
            if 0 <= index < len(self._tasks):
                self._tasks[index] = new_text
                return True
            return False

        @edit
        def mark_completed(self, index: int) -> bool:
            """Modify task by appending completion marker"""
            if 0 <= index < len(self._tasks):
                self._tasks[index] += " ✓"
                return True
            return False

        def data(self) -> list[str]:
            return self._tasks
    \endcode

    \section2 @move

    Marks a method that moves items within the model.

    \code [python]
    def move(func: Callable[..., Any]) -> Callable[..., Any]:
        """
        Decorator that marks a method as a *move* operation for QML-bound
        models.

        The method should accept two integer indices: from_index and to_index.
        """
    \endcode

    \b {Example:}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import move

    class TaskList:
        def __init__(self):
            self._tasks = ["First", "Second", "Third"]

        @move
        def move_task(self, from_index: int, to_index: int) -> bool:
            """Move task from one position to another"""
            if (0 <= from_index < len(self._tasks) and
                0 <= to_index < len(self._tasks)):
                task = self._tasks.pop(from_index)
                self._tasks.insert(to_index, task)
                return True
            return False

        def data(self) -> list[str]:
            return self._tasks
    \endcode

    \section2 @reset

    Marks a method that completely replaces the model data.

    \code [python]
    def reset(func: Callable[..., Any]) -> Callable[..., Any]:
        """
        Decorator that marks a method as a *reset* operation for QML-bound
        models.

        Use this when replacing the entire dataset or when you want to
        reload the entire data.
        """
    \endcode

    \b {Example:}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import reset

    class TaskList:
        def __init__(self):
            self._tasks = []

        @reset
        def load_tasks(self, tasks: list[str]) -> bool:
            """Replace all tasks with new list"""
            self._tasks = tasks.copy()
            return True

        @reset
        def load_from_file(self, filename: str) -> bool:
            """Load tasks from file, replacing current data"""
            try:
                with open(filename) as f:
                    self._tasks = [line.strip() for line in f]
                return True
            except Exception:
                return False

        def data(self) -> list[str]:
            return self._tasks
    \endcode

    \section2 @complete

    Marks a method as a component initialization handler for types registered
    with \c {bridge_type()}.

    \code [python]
    def complete(func: Callable[..., Any]) -> Callable[..., Any]:
        """
        Decorator that marks a method as a component initialization handler
        for types registered with bridge_type().

        This decorator corresponds to QQmlParserStatus::componentComplete() in Qt.
        When QML instantiates a Python type registered through bridge_type(),
        the method decorated with @complete is automatically called after the
        component is fully constructed and all properties have been set.
        """
    \endcode

    \b {When to Use:}

    The \c {@complete} decorator is essential when your Python class needs to
    perform initialization that depends on properties set by QML.

    \b {Important Notes:}

    \list
        \li Only works with types registered via \c {bridge_type()}, \b {not}
            \c {bridge_instance()}
        \li Called automatically by QML when the component is fully loaded
        \li Should \b {not} be called manually from your code
        \li Equivalent to \c {Component.onCompleted} in QML
        \li Called \b {after} all property bindings have been established
    \endlist

    \b {Example: REST Service Initialization}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import bridge_type, complete

    class RestService:
        def __init__(self):
            self._url = ""
            self._resources = []

        @property
        def url(self) -> str:
            return self._url

        @url.setter
        def url(self, value: str):
            self._url = value

        @property
        def resources(self) -> list:
            return self._resources

        @resources.setter
        def resources(self, value: list):
            self._resources = value

        @complete
        def componentComplete(self):
            """Called automatically after QML sets all properties"""
            # Initialize child resources with the configured URL
            for resource in self._resources:
                resource.set_base_url(self._url)

        def data(self) -> list:
            return []

    bridge_type(RestService, uri="backend", version="1.0")
    \endcode

    \b {QML Usage:}

    \code [qml]
    import backend 1.0

    RestService {
        url: "https://api.example.com"
        resources: [
            UsersResource { path: "/users" },
            ProductsResource { path: "/products" }
        ]
        // componentComplete() called here after properties are set
    }
    \endcode

    \b {Example: Data Validation}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import bridge_type, complete

    class DatabaseConnection:
        def __init__(self):
            self._host = ""
            self._database = ""

        @property
        def host(self) -> str:
            return self._host

        @host.setter
        def host(self, value: str):
            self._host = value

        @property
        def database(self) -> str:
            return self._database

        @database.setter
        def database(self, value: str):
            self._database = value

        @complete
        def componentComplete(self):
            """Validate configuration after properties are set"""
            if not self._host or not self._database:
                raise ValueError("Host and database are required")

        def data(self) -> list:
            return []

    bridge_type(DatabaseConnection, uri="backend", version="1.0")
    \endcode

    \b {QML Usage:}

    \code [qml]
    import backend 1.0

    DatabaseConnection {
        host: "localhost"
        database: "myapp"
        // componentComplete() validates here
    }
    \endcode

    \b {Comparison with Component.onCompleted in QML:}

    The \c {@complete} decorator in Python is equivalent to \c {Component.onCompleted} in QML:

    \code [qml]
    // QML approach
    Item {
        property string url: ""

        Component.onCompleted: {
            console.log("Component ready, URL is:", url)
            initializeWithUrl(url)
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \code [python]
    # Python approach with @complete
    from QtBridge import bridge_type, complete

    class MyComponent:
        def __init__(self):
            self._url = ""

        @property
        def url(self) -> str:
            return self._url

        @url.setter
        def url(self, value: str):
            self._url = value

        @complete
        def componentComplete(self):
            print(f"Component ready, URL is: {self._url}")
            self.initialize_with_url(self._url)

        def data(self) -> list:
            return []

    bridge_type(MyComponent, uri="backend", version="1.0")
    \endcode

    \section1 Property Binding and Signals

    Properties in Python classes are automatically exposed to QML with change signals (Note: add
    link to what are Singals in Qt/PySide). When a property is set from QML, the corresponding
    \c {<property>Changed} signal is emitted. This enables the user to communicate to QML to invoke
    specific functions when a value for a property changes.

    \section2 How It Works

    \list 1
        \li Define properties using `@property` decorator
        \li Provide setter methods
        \li QML automatically gets read/write access
        \li Change signals are generated automatically
    \endlist

    \section2 Example: Property-Driven UI Updates

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import bridge_instance, qtbridge

    class AppSettings:
        def __init__(self):
            self._theme = "light"
            self._font_size = 12
            self._username = "Guest"

        @property
        def theme(self) -> str:
            return self._theme

        @theme.setter
        def theme(self, value: str):
            """When QML sets this, themeChanged signal is emitted"""
            self._theme = value

        @property
        def font_size(self) -> int:
            return self._font_size

        @font_size.setter
        def font_size(self, value: int):
            """When QML sets this, fontSizeChanged signal is emitted"""
            self._font_size = value

        @property
        def username(self) -> str:
            return self._username

        @username.setter
        def username(self, value: str):
            """When QML sets this, usernameChanged signal is emitted"""
            self._username = value

        def data(self) -> list:
            return []

    @qtbridge(module="Main")
    def main():
        settings = AppSettings()
        bridge_instance(settings, name="Settings")
    \endcode

    \b {QML Usage:}

    \code [qml]
    import QtQuick
    import QtQuick.Controls
    import backend 1.0

    ApplicationWindow {
        // Properties automatically bind to Python
        title: "User: " + Settings.username

        Column {
            // UI automatically updates when property changes
            Text {
                text: "Current theme: " + Settings.theme
                font.pixelSize: Settings.font_size
            }

            // Setting property from QML triggers change signal
            Slider {
                from: 8
                to: 24
                value: Settings.font_size
                onValueChanged: Settings.font_size = value
            }

            ComboBox {
                model: ["light", "dark"]
                currentIndex: Settings.theme === "dark" ? 1 : 0
                onActivated: Settings.theme = currentText
            }

            TextField {
                text: Settings.username
                onEditingFinished: Settings.username = text
            }
        }

        // React to property changes
        Connections {
            target: Settings
            function onThemeChanged() {
                console.log("Theme changed to:", Settings.theme)
            }
            function onFontSizeChanged() {
                console.log("Font size changed to:", Settings.font_size)
            }
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \section2 Important Notes

    \b {Property Change Signals}: Change signals are only emitted when properties are set
    \b {from QML}. If you set a property from Python code, QML will \b {not} be notified automatically.
    For Python-initiated changes, consider using PySide6's \c {Signal()} mechanism.

    \section1 Best Practices

    \section2 1. Always Use Type Hints

    Type hints are \b {required} for QtBridges to correctly infer types:

    \section2 2. QML Names Must Start with Uppercase

    \code [python]
    bridge_instance(model, name="UserModel")
    bridge_instance(controller, name="App_Controller")
    \encode

    \section2 3. Use bridge_instance for Data, bridge_type for Components

    \code [python]
    class UserDatabase:
        def data(self) -> list[User]:
            return self._users

    bridge_instance(UserDatabase(), name="UserDB")

    class CustomButton:
        def data(self) -> list:
            return []

    bridge_type(CustomButton, uri="myapp.ui", version="1.0")
    \endcode

    \section2 4. Property Changes Only Trigger from QML

    Remember that property change signals only fire when set from QML:

    \code [python]
    class Settings:
        @property
        def theme(self) -> str:
            return self._theme

        @theme.setter
        def theme(self, value: str):
            self._theme = value
            # If you set self._theme from Python code,
            # QML won't be notified!
    \endcode

    For Python-initiated updates that need to notify QML, use PySide6's \c {Signal()} mechanism
    instead.

    \section2 5. Use DataClasses for Complex Models

    \code [python]
    from dataclasses import dataclass
    from typing import Optional

    @dataclass
    class Product:
        id: int
        name: str
        price: float
        stock: int
        category: str
        image_url: Optional[str] = None

